+// The exponentials of the softmax row, from XNNPACK's `f32-raddstoreexpminusmax` micro-kernels:
+// `avx2-rr2-p5`, `neonfma-rr1-p5` and `scalar-rr2-p5`, with their constants unchanged. On aarch64
+// XNNPACK's own config picks `neonfma-rr1-lut64-p2`; rr1_p5 measured faster on both chips here (whole
+// row, 1500 x 1500: A72 14.40 vs 14.69 ms, M1 1.09 vs 1.31 ms) at the same accuracy, with no table. The argument is always x - max <= 0, so neither
+// kernel handles overflow; everything below XNNPACK's denormal cutoff (-87.34, which includes the -inf
+// of a masked position) comes back as exactly 0 through a mask, NOT a branch.
+//
+// That mask is the reason for the port. `ggml_v_expf` is accurate everywhere (1.92 ulp worst case),
+// but it is written for the whole real line, so a vector holding any |n| > 126 leaves its fast path
+// for a three-way blend -- and a causal or padding mask puts -inf in half the row. On one AVX2 thread,
+// 1500 rows x 1500: 4.53 ms for dense rows and 7.39 ms for causal rows under the old loop; 3.01 ms
+// for causal rows with these kernels. On dense rows the two exponentials cost the same on AVX2.
+//
+// Accuracy over every exponent band of [-87, 0] plus 4M uniform points, against an f64 exp:
+//   avx2 / scalar rr2_p5  1.96 ulp max, 0.38 mean   (ggml_v_expf: 1.92 / 0.36, libm expf 0.50)
+//   neon rr1_p5           4.73 ulp max, 0.74 mean   (the max sits at -87, where the result is ~1e-38)
+// On a whole softmax row the exponential is not what the error is made of -- the sum is (below).
+//
